1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,600
This looks great on the graph,

2
00:00:02,750 --> 00:00:05,950
but how do we prove it works for every n?

3
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:08,700
Or rather, before that,
how do we even state it?

4
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:11,450
Well, the second question
is easy to answer.

5
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:15,600
We want to compare our
function with ln(n).

6
00:00:15,750 --> 00:00:17,750
So we divide one by the other,

7
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,600
and we want to say that this
ratio is very close to 1.

8
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:28,550
Of course, when n is small, our
statistics are not very good yet,

9
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:30,500
and there can be a deviation.

10
00:00:30,650 --> 00:00:33,850
But as n grows, the
deviation tends to vanish.

11
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,600
That is what the notion of
a limit was invented for.

12
00:00:36,750 --> 00:00:40,150
So our claim is really
that the limit is 1.

13
00:00:40,300 --> 00:00:43,300
And as we said, we can also
take any other percentage.

